From: AmberELEC
Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: run cleanup on rmmod (fix module-unload panic)

The Rockchip vendor exit function rockchip_wifi_exit_module_rtkwifi() has a
chip-type guard (CONFIG_WIFI_LOAD_DRIVER_WHEN_KERNEL_BOOTUP=y in the RG351
kernel config):

    if (type < WIFI_AP6XXX_SERIES || type == WIFI_ESP8089) return;

The RTL8188EU is an external USB dongle, so get_wifi_chip_type() (device-tree
"wifi_chip_type") returns a low/non-RTL value on the RG351MP. The guard then
early-returns and skips rtw_drv_halt() -> usb_deregister() -> disconnect
(rtw_dev_remove) -> usb_read_port_cancel(). The module is freed by the kernel
while the USB driver is still registered and RX urbs are still in flight; a
pending urb then completes into freed module text:

    Unable to handle kernel paging request ... [last unloaded: 8188eu]
    LR is at __usb_hcd_giveback_urb ... (usb_read_port_complete)

i.e. a panic + reboot on every rmmod / module reload. The matching guard in the
init path (rockchip_wifi_init_module_rtkwifi) is already commented out so the
dongle loads on this non-AP6XXX board; the exit guard was left active, which is
the asymmetry that breaks unload. Disable the exit guard too so the full
teardown always runs. Verified on RG351MP (RTL8188EUS 0bda:8179): rmmod +
modprobe now completes cleanly with no oops (dmesg shows rtw_drv_halt ->
rtw_dev_remove -> rtw_dev_unload -> "module exit success").

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/usb_intf.c	2026-08-30 09:42:30.652152273 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/usb_intf.c	2026-08-30 10:40:52.412998029 +0200
@@ -1649,10 +1649,17 @@
 
 void rockchip_wifi_exit_module_rtkwifi(void)
 {
+/* RTL8188EU is an external USB dongle; get_wifi_chip_type() returns a non-RTL
+ * value here, so this chip-type guard would early-return and skip rtw_drv_halt()
+ * (usb_deregister/disconnect/urb-cancel) on rmmod -> a still-pending RX urb then
+ * completes into freed module text and panics. The init path has the matching
+ * guard commented out for the same reason; keep exit symmetric so cleanup runs. */
+/*
 #ifdef CONFIG_WIFI_LOAD_DRIVER_WHEN_KERNEL_BOOTUP
     int type = get_wifi_chip_type();
     if (type < WIFI_AP6XXX_SERIES || type == WIFI_ESP8089) return;
 #endif
+*/
     printk("\n");
     printk("=======================================================\n");
     printk("==== Dislaunching Wi-Fi driver! (Powered by Rockchip) ====\n");
